      {
        "type": "twist",
        "title": "Quiero ir or quiero que vayas",
        "a": [
          "Quiero ir.",
          "I want to go."
        ],
        "b": [
          "Quiero que vayas.",
          "I want you to go."
        ],
        "note": "The same person wants and goes: no que, just ir. Someone else goes: que + subjuntivo."
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "kind": "warn",
        "text": "A very common slip: quiero que tú vienes. After quiero que, use the subjuntivo: quiero que vengas."
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "kind": "culture",
        "text": "Ojalá comes from Arabic and means something like God willing. Spaniards say it all the time, with the subjuntivo: ¡Ojalá haga sol mañana! Wishes with que are everyday too: que aproveche (enjoy your meal), que te mejores (get well soon)."
      }
    ]
